A||N||10||jeffreyeffrey||when is enough enough?||||9||
Z||000000||jeffreyeffrey||04-23-2001||02:44 PM||||After a two year hiatus, I decided to get my '86 GT back out on the road, the only reason I quit driving it in '99 was because the (3rd) motor was starting to miss and use oil again. Last week I got it legal to drive and decided to get it to the shop and get a status report. I was phoned a few hours ago and was told the 3rd and 5th cylinders are fried. So you know what that means, motor #4!!! I would just like to know what in the hell is making these delco rebuilds go after 30,000 miles? I admit I don't drive it like a granny, but I don't beat it to death either. I live on a gravel road and have to drive on it for at least a mile anytime I take it anywhere, which makes the poorly placed intake suck a lot of dust, but I checked the air cleaner all the time and blew it out nearly every 2 weeks and replaced it every 2500 miles when I drove it every day. I Love the car to death and hate to see it in disrepair, but after all this I'm getting fed up, After 6 years and est. $12,000 I still have a bucket of bolts with 145,000 miles on it. I guess I'm just wondering is there any tips on how to keep dust out of the motor better, and if I decide to put #4 in, does anyone recomend any remanufactured motors other than delco?||||reg||9
Z||000001||stimpy||04-23-2001||03:39 PM||||Damn, almost cheaper to pave the gravel road!<P>I don't think I'd want to go the route of having a Delco rebuild either.  I know that Jasper and Grooms have a good rep for rebuilt motors, or you could find a local reputable machine shop to rebuild your current engine. Not that it would be a whole lot cheaper, but many shops will warranty their work. <BR>Also, get yerself a K&N air filter. Sounds like that would save you some money too.<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||KEV||04-23-2001||04:11 PM||||sorry to hear about your motor problems. to let you know if you need a new motor i can get you a new motor from Brookdale Pontiac in Brooklyn Park Mn for $1200 with a 3yr/36,000 mile warrenty, and i would use a K&N filter a much better filter then stock ones.  Events Chairmen for the Minnesota Fieros Forever club.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sparks||04-23-2001||04:17 PM||||To contradict what both the gentlemen mentioned before me I'd caution you against the K&N with the gravel road.  I drive my Jeep Wrangler ~5 miles to and from work and sometimes more during the summer when it's dry and dusty.  I quite using my K&N after seeing all the dust that made it past it into the air tube (yes it was cleaned and oiled properly).  That said, if I were to use a conical K&N with a prefilter I think you'd be a lot better off but with a stock air box the best I can do is my stock element with the oiled foam on the bottom before the paper element.<P>Just my $.02<P>------------------<P>Adam Sparks<p>[This message has been edited by sparks (edited 04-23-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000004||fierojunky||04-23-2001||04:23 PM||||instructions that came with my K&N Gen II system for my truck  "CAUTION: Extreme fine dust - agriculture or off-road use - will pull the oil from the element. Frequent re-oiling of the element's clean side might be required. Completely service when acticable. For extra protection use K&N sealing grease on rubber ends of the element. Service only with K&N air filter cleaner and K&N air filter oil."  If you get the K&N - make sure to check OFTEN with that driveway!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bob Englert||04-23-2001||05:49 PM||||If you do have the replace the motor again, Grooms offers a lifetime warranty for the original purchaser for and extra $100 or so.  They will replace the motor (Probably does not include labor) no questions asked if it becomes necessary.  This is above their normal 18 month warranty. <P>I paid the extra to get this warranty coverage on my daughter's 2.5L that Ed Parks installed last year.  It is available only when installing the same type as originally in the car.  Does not apply to 3.4L upgrades.<P>You might want to give Ed Parks a call and ask him about it.  He is a dealer for Grooms.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||04-23-2001||07:19 PM||||You said the #3 and #5 are fried. does that mean the cylinder walls? Or are the pistons fried?<BR>What I'm getting at... Is your EGR operating properly?<BR>One of Ed Parks observations was that every V-6 that he's ever seen that ate those two (I think) pistons, had had the EGR disabled. <BR>I would encourage you to give him a call, as Bob suggested. Ed comes highly recommended by most of us. <A HREF="http://welcome.to/thefierofactory" TARGET=_blank>http://welcome.to/thefierofactory</A>  <P>It's possible that this post will start a flame war. There are many differing opinions on this EGR thing. This is merely mine.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||04-24-2001||03:15 PM||||maybe since your frying so many motors, your not doing proper break-in in the proper way. The first couple thousand miles are the most important to longevity. Ive put 40K+ on my 3.1 without any kind of problem. And its turboed, K&N filtered, basicly same as a 2.8 I keep my revs high in power band, and I cant even MAKE it use ANY oil. I drive it almost everyday, all kinds of weather, in Ohio. Not even a slight tick anywhere in motor. Thats why Im going ahead with another kitcar, mines been very reliable.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||04-24-2001||05:23 PM|||| Most engine rebuilding shops who mass produce these remanufactured engines go the cheapest way by reusing old parts or cheap new parts and what you end up with is a piece of junk that only lasts 30,000 miles and then quits.<P>I would suggest you rebuild it yourself or have a reputable machine shop do it for you and use good parts. Also check to see if you’re #3 and #5 fuel injectors are clogged. Maybe you should have them blueprinted by <A HREF="http://www.injector.com" TARGET=_blank>www.injector.com</A>    <P>Why waste the time blowing dirt out of an $8 paper air filter risking getting dirt into the engine when its so much easier and safer to just replace it?<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||sparks||04-25-2001||11:34 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierojunky:<BR><B>instructions that came with my K&N Gen II system for my truck  "CAUTION: Extreme fine dust - agriculture or off-road use - will pull the oil from the element. Frequent re-oiling of the element's clean side might be required. Completely service when acticable. For extra protection use K&N sealing grease on rubber ends of the element. Service only with K&N air filter cleaner and K&N air filter oil."  If you get the K&N - make sure to check OFTEN with that driveway!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmm, maybe the grease around the outside and oiling it more would help.  But it is extremely fine dust, permates everything even with the hard top on I get dust all over inside the Jeep.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||ironhedno3||04-25-2001||03:49 PM||||hi folks..im keith the guy that ed got all his remans from through grooms..best i can remeber is that gm was recalling a bunch of thier reman stuff due to incorrect piston to piston wall tolerances..dont know if this would apply to you but its a thought..i do know that it mainly applied to the mexican built goodwrench line that was built in mexico..mainly 2.5/2.8.3.1 and 5.7s..i would also have to aggree with the egr situation..its on there for a reason..mainly to lean the fuel mix and also to cool the combustion chamber||||reg||1
